  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Trooping Crumble Cap is a small, delicate mushroom with a distinctive bell-shaped cap that is gray to brownish and becomes radially grooved as it matures. Despite its fragile appearance, this species is notable for fruiting in large clusters or troops, hence its common name.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap appearance",
      "description": "Small bell-shaped cap (0.5-1.5 cm) that is gray-brown and becomes deeply radially grooved or pleated from the center outward",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gills and spore deposit",
      "description": "Free gills that are initially white then darken with age; produces a black spore print",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruiting pattern",
      "description": "Fruits in dense troops or clusters on decaying wood, stumps, and leaf litter rather than solitary specimens",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found throughout New England on dead hardwood logs, stumps, decaying wood chips, and rich forest soil in deciduous and mixed forests.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The caps of Trooping Crumble Caps are so delicate and ephemeral that they often dissolve or crumble away within hours of fruiting, disappearing like morning dew despite appearing in spectacular troops of hundreds or thousands of specimens.",
